# HQL-0064: child processes

Companion to [[HQL-0064]]. A **child process** is another program that a
script starts, writes to, reads from and waits for. This note compares how
Python, Haskell and Rust start one, and defines the operations of
`io.process` that do it in HQL. The companion note
[advanced child processes](03-advanced-child-processes.hmd) continues it
with the child's pipes wired to channels, and the
[I/O FAQ](faq.hmd) shows the programs that hang or fail and why.

!!! warning "Design only"
    Nothing in this note is implemented, and [[HQL-0064]] does not deploy
    it. The declarations are a design direction. They extend the module of
    the companion note [`io.process`](01-io-process.hmd) under the rules of
    the note [pure and effectful](../../HQL-0062/parts/01-pure-and-effectful.hmd) of [[HQL-0062]].

!!! abstract "TL;DR"
    - **Python: `subprocess`.** One call starts the program. A shell is one
      argument away, and a non-zero exit status is ignored unless the
      caller asks for an exception. HQL takes neither.
    - **Haskell: `System.Process`.** The process is described by a value,
      `CreateProcess`, before it is run. HQL takes that. It does not take
      the four handles the run gives back, or the two sets of functions
      that differ in whether a failure throws.
    - **Rust: `std::process::Command`.** A value describes the process,
      arguments are a list that no shell reads, and the exit status is a
      value. HQL takes all three.
    - **HQL: `command`.** A `Command` is a pure value and an endpoint, read
      and written by the four operations of `io`: `source` waits for the
      child and gives everything it wrote, `tap` gives it as it arrives,
      `target` writes one value to its standard input and `sink` writes a
      stream to it. `run` waits and gives a `Result`.
    - **Two rules are HQL's own.** A non-zero exit status is a failure, and
      a child's lifetime is bounded by the evaluation that started it.

## The four approaches

| | The process is a value before it runs | A shell reads the command | A non-zero exit status is | A child still running at the end is |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Python | no | when `shell=True` | ignored, or an exception with `check=True` | left running |
| Haskell | yes, `CreateProcess` | with `shell`, not with `proc` | an exception or a value, by the function called | left running |
| Rust | yes, `Command` | never | a value, `ExitStatus` | left running |
| HQL | yes, `Command` | never | a failure | terminated |

## Python: `subprocess`

```python
import subprocess

done = subprocess.run(["git", "log", "--format=%an"],
                      capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)
authors = done.stdout.splitlines()
```

- `run` starts the program, waits for it, and gives a `CompletedProcess`
  with the status and what was captured. `Popen` starts it without
  waiting, and gives an object whose pipes the caller reads and writes.
- With `shell=True` the command is one string and `/bin/sh` reads it. A
  value from outside the program that is pasted into that string is run as
  shell text.
- A non-zero status is ignored by default. `check=True` raises
  `CalledProcessError`.
- A `Popen` whose two pipes are read and written by hand deadlocks when
  either fills. `communicate()` exists to avoid that.

HQL does not take the shell argument, the ignored status or the exception.

## Haskell: `System.Process`

```haskell
import System.Process

main :: IO ()
main = do
  out <- readCreateProcess (proc "git" ["log", "--format=%an"]) ""
  mapM_ putStrLn (lines out)
```

- `proc` and `shell` each give a `CreateProcess`, a record that describes
  the process: the program, its arguments, its directory, its environment
  and what each standard stream is connected to. Building one starts
  nothing.
- `createProcess` runs it and gives four values: three `Maybe Handle`, one
  for each standard stream, and a `ProcessHandle`.
- `readProcess` throws an exception on a non-zero status.
  `readProcessWithExitCode` gives the status as a value instead. The caller
  chooses failure handling by choosing the function.

HQL takes the description as a value. It does not take the four handles,
which a caller must each close, or the pairs of functions.

## Rust: `std::process::Command`

```rust
use std::process::Command;

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let out = Command::new("git").args(["log", "--format=%an"]).output()?;
    if out.status.success() {
        print!("{}", String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stdout));
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

- `Command` is a value built in steps: `new`, `arg`, `env`, `current_dir`,
  `stdin`. Nothing runs until `spawn`, `status` or `output`.
- Arguments are a list. No shell reads them, so an argument that holds a
  space or a `;` is one argument.
- `output()` waits and gives `Output`: the status, standard output and
  standard error. `spawn()` gives a `Child` whose streams the caller reads
  and writes.
- The `Err` of these calls states that the program did not start. A
  non-zero status is not an `Err`. It is an `ExitStatus` inside `Ok`, and
  the caller tests `success()`.
- A `Child` that is dropped is not killed and not waited for.

HQL takes the value, the argument list and the status as a value. It
differs on the last two points, as the rules below state.

## HQL: `command`

```hql
module io.process

/// A program to run, with its arguments. Constructing one starts nothing.
/// Read, it is the child's standard output; written, its standard input.
/// Each read or write starts the program once, as each read of an `Http`
/// endpoint is one request.
type Command : {Replayable, Writable} {
    program : String
    args    : List<String>
}

/// What a child that exited with status `0` wrote.
type Output {
    stdout : String
    stderr : String
}

/// Why a command did not succeed. `stdout` and `stderr` hold what the
/// child wrote before it ended.
type CommandFailure : DataContract {
    command : Command
    reason  : String
    status? : Int
    signal? : Signal
    stdout  : String
    stderr  : String
}

/// The command that runs `program` with `args`. `dir` is the child's
/// working directory, this process's own unless given. `env`, a map of
/// names to text, is added to the environment the child inherits. A child
/// still running after `timeout_ms` milliseconds is terminated; `0` sets
/// no bound.
fn command(program: String, args: List<String> = [], *, dir: String = "",
           env: Data = {:}, timeout_ms: Int = 0) : Command

/// Starts the command, writes `input` to its standard input, waits for it
/// to end, and gives what it wrote.
effectful fn run(self: Command, input: String = "") : Result<Output, CommandFailure>
```

`source`, `tap`, `target` and `sink` need no declaration of their own.
`Command` is `Replayable` and `Writable`, so the four operations of `io`
accept it.

### The five operations

| Operation | The child's standard input | The child's standard output | `hql` waits for the child | Gives |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `source(c, F)` | closed | read to its end, decoded by `F` | yes | `Seq<T>`, every document |
| `tap(c, F)` | closed | read as it arrives, decoded by `F` | no | `Stream<T>` |
| `target(v, c, F)` | `v` as one document, then closed | this process's standard output | yes | `Step` |
| `sink(vs, c, F)` | each of `vs` as it arrives, closed when `vs` ends | this process's standard output | yes | `Step` |
| `c.run(input)` | `input`, then closed | captured | yes | `Result<Output, CommandFailure>` |

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.process import *

git = command("git", ["log", "--format=%an"])

// source: wait for git to end, then every line it wrote.
authors = source(git, Lines)
authors | distinct | size

// tap: each line as git writes it. The first 10 end the read.
tap(git, Lines) | take(10)

// target: one document on the child's standard input.
{name: "wren", count: 3} | target(command("jq", [".count"]), Json)

// sink: one document for each value. It closes standard input when the
// values end: after the file's last line, or never, for a live tap.
source(file("names.txt"), Lines) | sink(command("sort"), Lines)
tap(git, Lines) | sink(command("tr", ["a-z", "A-Z"]), Lines)

// run: wait, and handle the failure.
match git.run() {
    ok(out) => out.stdout,
    err(e)  => fmt("git failed: {}", e.stderr),
}
```

- `source(c, F)` MUST start the child, read its standard output to its end,
  wait for it, and give every document decoded by `F` as a `Seq<T>`.
- `tap(c, F)` MUST start the child and give each document as it arrives,
  as a `Stream<T>`.
- `target(v, c, F)` MUST start the child, write `v` encoded by `F` as one
  document, close the child's standard input, and wait.
- `sink(vs, c, F)` MUST start the child, write each value of `vs` encoded
  by `F` as one document as the value arrives, close the child's standard
  input when `vs` ends, and wait. A `vs` that does not end never closes
  it, so `sink` returns only when the child ends by itself or is
  terminated.
- `run` MUST start the child, write `input`, close the child's standard
  input, read its standard output and standard error to their ends, and
  wait.

`target` writes one document and `sink` one document for each value. Both
close standard input when their input ends, so a list or `source(file(…),
Lines)` ends under either. What a child needs is decided by the input, not
by the operation. A child that reads its whole input before it writes, as
`sort`, `wc` and `jq` do, answers only when its input ends, so a `sink`
from a stream that does not end leaves it waiting. A child that answers
each line as it reads it, as `tr`, `sed` and `bc` do, also works behind a
live `tap`.
- Each operation on one `Command` value MUST start one child. Two `source`
  calls are two runs of the program.

### The child's three pipes

A child has three pipes: standard input, standard output and standard
error. The operation that starts the child reads or writes one of them,
its **primary pipe**. The other two are **secondary**.

| Operation | Primary pipe | Secondary pipes |
|---|---|---|
| `source`, `tap` of `c` or `c.stdout` | standard output | standard input, standard error |
| `source`, `tap` of `c.stderr` | standard error | standard input, standard output |
| `target`, `sink` | standard input | standard output, standard error |
| `run` | none: it writes `input` and captures both outputs | none |

- A secondary standard input MUST be closed when the child starts, so the
  child reads an empty input.
- A secondary standard output MUST go to this process's standard output.
- A secondary standard error MUST go to this process's standard error.
- `hql` MUST read every output pipe as the child writes it. A child MUST
  NOT block on a full pipe that the program has not yet read.

A secondary pipe is given another destination by wiring it to a channel,
and standard error is made the primary pipe of a read with the field
`stderr`, `tap(command(…).stderr, F)`.
Both are defined in the companion note
[advanced child processes](03-advanced-child-processes.hmd).

### The command line

- The child MUST receive `program` and `args` as written. No shell reads
  them: no word is split, no `*` is expanded and no `$NAME` is replaced.
- A `program` without `/` MUST be looked up in `PATH`. A relative `program`
  with `/` MUST resolve against the directory of the source unit that names
  it, as every path does ([[HQL-0059]]).
- A pattern of file names is expanded in HQL, with `glob` of `io.fs`, and
  each match is one argument:

  ```hql
  txt = folder(working_dir()) | glob(Glob("*.txt")) | map(e => e.name)
  command("wc", concat(["-l"], txt))
  ```

- A script that needs shell text names the shell as the program:
  `command("sh", ["-c", line])`. `Command` has no argument that turns a
  shell on.

### Failures

A command fails in one of eight ways. `reason` names it: the field of
`CommandFailure` under `run`, and the failure that stops evaluation under
`source`, `tap`, `target` and `sink`.

| `reason` | The failure | Set beside it |
|---|---|---|
| `not-found` | no program of that name, in `PATH` or at the path | |
| `not-permitted` | the file is not executable by this user | |
| `exited` | the child exited with a status other than `0` | `status` |
| `signalled` | a signal ended the child | `signal` |
| `timed-out` | `timeout_ms` passed and `hql` terminated the child | |
| `closed` | under `target` or `sink`, the child closed its standard input before a write ended | |
| `unhealthy` | its watchdog gave up and `hql` terminated the child ([watchdogs](06-watchdog.hmd)) | |
| `invalid-text` | under `run`, the child wrote bytes that are not UTF-8, and `invalid_utf8` is `fail` | |

A child that does not exit `0` is a failure the program can handle, so
`run` gives it as a `Result` ([[HQL-0033]]).

- `run` MUST give `ok(Output)` when the child exits `0`, and
  `err(CommandFailure)` for each failure of the table.
- `run` MUST judge the child by how it ended. A child that exits without
  reading its `input` is not `closed`, so no `CommandFailure` has the
  reason `closed`.
- `closed` states that a write was refused, not that every line was read.
  Lines the pipe already holds when the child exits without reading them are
  lost without a failure, so whether a `target` or `sink` to such a child
  fails depends on the size of the input and on timing
  ([the FAQ](faq.hmd#why-does-a-target-to-head-sometimes-fail-with-closed)).
- `source`, `tap`, `target` and `sink` give a sequence, a stream and a
  `Step`, which hold no status. A failure under them MUST stop evaluation
  with an unrecoverable failure that states the command, the reason, the
  status and the last lines of the child's standard error. A program that
  handles the failure uses `run`.
- A document of the child's output that `F` does not decode is a failure of
  the format, as it is for a file, and not a `CommandFailure`.
- A command the grant refuses is a forbidden effect, an unrecoverable
  failure, under every operation.
- A program whose last expression is `command(…).run()` exits `1` when the
  child fails, by the section "The exit status" of [[HQL-0062]].

!!! note "Where this differs from Rust"
    Rust gives a non-zero status inside `Ok`, and the caller tests
    `success()`. HQL gives it as `err`, so the output of a failed child is
    reached only through a `match` or `map_err`. A tool that uses a
    non-zero status for an ordinary answer, as `grep` uses `1` for no
    match, is handled by matching `e.status`.

### Text a child writes

A child writes bytes; `Output`, `CommandFailure` and every text format
give `String`, which holds only UTF-8. What `hql` does with bytes that are
not UTF-8 is a setting of `.hql/config.toml` ([[HQL-0059]]):

```toml
[command_output]
invalid_utf8 = "fail"      # fail | replace
```

| `invalid_utf8` | Bytes that are not UTF-8 |
|---|---|
| `fail`, the default | under `run`, `err(CommandFailure)` with the reason `invalid-text`; under `source`, `tap`, `target` and `sink`, a failure of the format |
| `replace` | each invalid sequence becomes U+FFFD, the replacement character, and the read goes on |

- Absent, `invalid_utf8` MUST be `fail`.
- The setting MUST NOT change a type. Every value a child's output gives
  is a `String` under either setting.
- The setting applies to standard output and standard error, and to the
  lines of a pipe wired to a channel.

### The child's environment and lifetime

A child's lifetime is bounded by the evaluation that started it.

- `hql` MUST start each child as the leader of a new process group. Every
  process the child starts, and they start, belongs to that group unless
  it leaves it.
- Termination, on every path below, MUST signal the child's process group,
  not only the child. A `make` that `hql` terminates takes the `cc` it
  started with it.

- The child MUST inherit the environment variables this program is granted
  by `env`, and no other. A child would otherwise read what the `env` grant
  withholds from the program ([[HQL-0041]]).
- When `timeout_ms` passes, `hql` MUST terminate the child.
- When the evaluation ends with a child still running, on a failure or
  because a stream was not read to its end, `hql` MUST terminate the child
  and wait for it. Termination is `SIGTERM`, then `SIGKILL` after 5
  seconds.
- `hql` MUST terminate its children when a signal ends `hql` itself.

```d2
direction: down

value: "command(program, args)\na pure value"
grant: "commands grant\npermits or refuses the program"
start: "source, tap, target, sink or run\nstarts the child"
child: "child process\nreads stdin, writes stdout and stderr"
end: "the child ends"
ok: "exit 0\nthe value, or ok(Output)"
err: "any other end\nevaluation stops, or err(CommandFailure)"

value -> grant
grant -> start
start -> child
child -> end
end -> ok
end -> err
```

### The grant

Starting a program gives a script every right of the user who runs it, so
a grant names the programs. It is a top-level key of `.hql/config.toml`, beside
`env` and `process` ([[HQL-0059]]):

```toml
commands = ["git", "kubectl"]
```

- Absent, a run MUST grant every program. Stated, it grants the programs
  it lists, each matched against `program` as written, and no other.
- A shell is granted like any other program. A list without `sh`, `bash`
  or another shell keeps shell text out of the project: no command a
  script builds is read by a shell. Listing one is the project's decision
  to run shell text.

```toml
commands = ["git", "wc"]        # no shell
commands = ["git", "wc", "sh"]  # sh -c is permitted
```
- `hql render` MUST grant no program.

### Windows

`hql` runs children on Windows through the Win32 API itself. It needs no
POSIX layer, no MSYS, Cygwin or WSL, and no shell.

| Unix | Windows |
|---|---|
| spawn with `posix_spawn` or `fork` and `exec` | `CreateProcessW` |
| `program` looked up in `PATH` | the same; `git` finds `git.exe` |
| one argument vector | one command line, quoted by `hql` from `args` by the rules the Microsoft C runtime parses |
| a new process group | a **Job Object** that kills its processes when closed |
| terminate: `SIGTERM` to the group, `SIGKILL` after 5 seconds | a graceful stop, then `TerminateJobObject` after 5 seconds |
| measurements summed over the group | the Job Object's accounting, for the whole tree |

- `hql` MUST start each child in a new Job Object, closed when `hql`
  closes. Every process the child starts belongs to the job.
- The graceful stop MUST send Ctrl-Break to a console child, started
  with a console process group of its own, and `WM_CLOSE` to each
  top-level window of a process in the job that has one.
- A child that `hql` terminated reports the reason of the termination
  (`timed-out`, `unhealthy`) as on Unix. A child that exits by itself with
  a non-zero code is `exited`; Windows sets no `signal`.
- A `program` ending in `.bat` or `.cmd` is run by `cmd.exe`, a shell. The
  grant MUST refuse it unless `commands` lists both the file and `cmd`.
- An argument that `hql` cannot quote so that `cmd.exe` reads it as one
  argument MUST be the evaluation failure `invalid-argument`, which names
  the argument.

**Implementation.** The crate keeps `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`. Every call
into the Win32 API that needs `unsafe`, the Job Object, its accounting,
`WM_CLOSE`, is in one internal workspace crate, `crates/hql-sys`, on
`windows-sys`, which exposes a safe API and nothing else. Spawning, pipes
and console events use `std` and `tokio`, which need no `unsafe` in the
crate.

**Tests.** CI MUST build and test on Windows beside Linux and macOS. A
test that needs a child starts the test binary itself, chosen by an
environment variable, or `hql` on a small script; no test needs `sort`,
`tr`, `bc` or another Unix tool. The examples of this proposal use those
tools, and run on Unix.

!!! tip "Windows: a Unix name may be another program"
    `sort`, `find` and `timeout` are programs of Windows itself, with
    other options. `command("sort")` on Windows runs `sort.exe` from
    `System32`. Name a program by its path, or grant it by name in
    `commands`, to know which one runs.

!!! tip "Windows: a child does not outlive `hql`"
    The Job Object ends every process in it when `hql` ends, even when
    `hql` is killed. On Unix a process group outlives an `hql` ended by
    `SIGKILL`.

### Purity

- `command` is pure. It builds a value and reaches nothing, as `Path` and
  `tcp` do.
- `run`, and the four operations of `io` on a `Command`, are effectful. The
  planner keeps them in program order and does not repeat one that has
  completed ([[HQL-0055]]).
